{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman who played a significant role in the history of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. Born around 1140 he was the second son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His family belonged to the prestigious House of Toulouse which had strong ties to the Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond III ascended to the title of count after his brother Bohemond III died without leaving a male heir in 1199 although some sources suggest his rule began slightly earlier due to joint governance with Bohemond III.\n\n*Early life and family*\nRaymond III was part of a powerful dynasty that had deep roots in both Europe and the Holy Land. His mother Hodierna was a prominent figure in her own right as the daughter of King Fulk of Jerusalem and Queen Melisende. This lineage connected Raymond to the highest echelons of Crusader nobility. His brother Bohemond III had married Princess Theodora Komnene daughter of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos further cementing ties between the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire. Raymond’s upbringing reflected the multicultural and multilingual environment of the Levant where Latin Christian Franks interacted with Greeks Armenians and Muslims.\n\nDuring his reign Raymond III faced numerous challenges including the expansionist policies of Saladin’s successors and the political instability within the Crusader states. He maintained a cautious approach focusing on diplomacy and alliances rather than military confrontation. One of his notable diplomatic achievements was his marriage to Eschiva of Bures daughter of Hugh II of Bures who brought significant wealth and influence to the County of Tripoli. This union helped secure the county’s position amidst the turbulent political landscape of the region.\n\n*Military campaigns and alliances*\nRaymond III’s military involvement was limited compared to his predecessors but he was not entirely passive. He participated in key events such as the Battle of Arsuf in 1191 where he fought alongside Richard the Lionheart though his exact role remains unclear. His strategies often revolved around fortifying Tripolitanian borders and negotiating with local Muslim rulers. He also had to contend with internal threats from rival Crusader lords and the threat of the Egyptian Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin’s successors.\n\nOne of his more notable actions was his support for the Fifth Crusade. Raymond III played a crucial role in facilitating the movement of Crusader forces through his territories which were critical for the campaign’s logistics. His cooperation with other Crusader leaders helped maintain a fragile unity among the fragmented states of the Levant. Despite his efforts the political climate remained precarious as the Crusader states faced growing isolation and internal divisions.\n\n*Legacy and death*\nRaymond III’s legacy is often overshadowed by his more prominent predecessors and contemporaries like Saladin and Richard the Lionheart. However his reign was marked by a period of relative stability within the County of Tripoli. He managed to keep the county viable despite the broader decline of Crusader power in the region. His diplomatic skills and ability to navigate complex alliances were crucial in preserving Tripolitanian autonomy.\n\nHe died in 1216 after a reign that lasted nearly two decades. His demise marked the end of an era for the House of Toulouse in Tripoli. His daughter Alice succeeded him but her reign was short-lived as she died in 1224 without leaving an heir. This led to the county being passed to Raymond-Roupen a distant cousin who belonged to a different branch of the family. Raymond III’s story reflects the broader challenges and strategies of Crusader nobles during the final phases of the medieval Crusades. His life illustrates the balance between military action diplomacy and survival in a rapidly changing region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.07479121536016464}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001674750237725675}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0035772635601460934}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.22211115062236786}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0003123987407889217}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.016026604920625687}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4773028790950775}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.1037449985742569}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.3610548973083496}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -8.797258487902582e-05}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.5600115060806274}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.11467017233371735}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.9699777960777283}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -1.2180534601211548}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.030659407377243042}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -0.24991486966609955}, {"token": " role", "logprob": -0.017741788178682327}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.82725989818573}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.01732618547976017}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -0.2325565069913864}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00012885693286079913}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.010677115991711617}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.9664711356163025}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.823885577323381e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.006251778919249773}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.3130700886249542}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.004462759476155043}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1960186958312988}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.892617915989831e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.027019867673516273}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -2.777537883957848e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -0.021087490022182465}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.07921358197927475}, {"token": " Born", "logprob": -0.5846275091171265}, {"token": " around", "logprob": -0.5086716413497925}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.16083115339279175}, {"token